1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,970
<font color="#000000">Contrast-Consistent Search, or CCS,</font>

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:09,601
<font color="#000000">is an unsupervised probe that reads a language
model's sense of truth from its activations.</font>

3
00:00:09,681 --> 00:00:12,225
<font color="#000000">But what does CCS actually optimize?</font>

4
00:00:12,305 --> 00:00:17,835
<font color="#000000">This paper explains its target and derives a new
loss, Midpoint-Displacement,</font>

5
00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:20,240
<font color="#000000">that matches CCS and can beat it.</font>

6
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:26,967
<font color="#000000">CCS recovers a direction in a model's activations
encoding whether a statement is true or false,</font>

7
00:00:27,047 --> 00:00:32,322
<font color="#000000">using no labels, only the constraint that a
statement and its negation disagree.</font>

8
00:00:32,402 --> 00:00:36,205
<font color="#000000">Yet nobody had pinned down what its loss really optimizes,</font>

9
00:00:36,285 --> 00:00:38,280
<font color="#000000">or whether that target is best.</font>

10
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:43,115
<font color="#000000">Safely deploying capable models means catching
confident falsehoods,</font>

11
00:00:43,195 --> 00:00:47,583
<font color="#000000">and probes that read a model's own truth
representation could help,</font>

12
00:00:47,663 --> 00:00:49,650
<font color="#000000">but only if we understand them.</font>

13
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:54,718
<font color="#000000">CCS was explained through clustering activations
and learning probabilities.</font>

14
00:00:54,798 --> 00:01:00,320
<font color="#000000">The authors argue both pictures mislead, motivating
a cleaner account of its target.</font>

15
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,326
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

16
00:01:04,406 --> 00:01:08,031
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

17
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:11,737
<font color="#000000">it corrects two misconceptions: CCS classifies
using only the displacement between a statement and its negation,</font>

18
00:01:11,817 --> 00:01:15,443
<font color="#000000">needing no separating hyperplane,</font>

19
00:01:15,523 --> 00:01:19,149
<font color="#000000">and succeeds even when probabilities cluster near
one half.</font>

20
00:01:19,229 --> 00:01:22,854
<font color="#000000">Second, it derives a new Midpoint-Displacement loss.</font>

21
00:01:22,934 --> 00:01:26,560
<font color="#000000">Third, a comparison shows it proxies, and can beat, CCS.</font>

22
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:32,338
<font color="#000000">CCS is described by two statistics along the probe
direction: sigma-d-squared,</font>

23
00:01:32,418 --> 00:01:37,605
<font color="#000000">how far a statement and its negation are pushed
apart, and sigma-m-squared,</font>

24
00:01:37,685 --> 00:01:40,414
<font color="#000000">how far their midpoint sits from origin.</font>

25
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:44,487
<font color="#000000">Its saturating sigmoid forces a trade-off between the two.</font>

26
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:48,981
<font color="#000000">Midpoint-Displacement makes this explicit with one
knob, lambda,</font>

27
00:01:49,061 --> 00:01:51,720
<font color="#000000">reproducing CCS or maximizing accuracy.</font>

28
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:59,743
<font color="#000000">Experiments use hidden-state activations from four
models: the encoder and decoder of UnifiedQA T5-Large,</font>

29
00:01:59,823 --> 00:02:04,597
<font color="#000000">DeBERTa, and GPT-Neo, averaged over five datasets
including BoolQ.</font>

30
00:02:04,677 --> 00:02:07,833
<font color="#000000">Contrast pairs append two exclusive answers,</font>

31
00:02:07,913 --> 00:02:13,570
<font color="#000000">each set normalized independently so the probe
cannot detect the answer token.</font>

32
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:20,057
<font color="#000000">The headline: Midpoint-Displacement tuned to
imitate CCS gives probe directions averaging</font>

33
00:02:20,137 --> 00:02:24,506
<font color="#000000">cosine similarity about zero point six three to
real CCS probes,</font>

34
00:02:24,586 --> 00:02:28,676
<font color="#000000">while CCS agrees with itself only at zero point
seven eight.</font>

35
00:02:28,756 --> 00:02:32,847
<font color="#000000">Other accurate losses like PCA sit near zero point
one five,</font>

36
00:02:32,927 --> 00:02:36,740
<font color="#000000">so the resemblance is specific to Midpoint-Displacement.</font>

37
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:41,506
<font color="#000000">The two Midpoint-Displacement variants differ only
in lambda.</font>

38
00:02:41,586 --> 00:02:46,485
<font color="#000000">Tuned to mimic CCS, cosine similarity is about zero
point six three;</font>

39
00:02:46,565 --> 00:02:50,512
<font color="#000000">tuned for accuracy, it drops to zero point three eight,</font>

40
00:02:50,592 --> 00:02:55,784
<font color="#000000">showing the displacement-versus-midpoint trade-off
defines CCS's target.</font>

41
00:02:55,864 --> 00:03:00,250
<font color="#000000">Retuning lambda raises test accuracy to zero point
seven six.</font>

42
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:08,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers: Midpoint-Displacement matched to CCS
reaches average cosine similarity about zero point six three,</font>

43
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:12,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus CCS self-similarity zero point seven eight,</font>

44
00:03:12,098 --> 00:03:15,817
<font color="#FFFFFF">while competing losses reach only zero point one five.</font>

45
00:03:15,897 --> 00:03:22,431
<font color="#FFFFFF">The accuracy-tuned variant averages zero point
seven six accuracy versus zero point seven one,</font>

46
00:03:22,511 --> 00:03:26,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">winning on three of four models, across five datasets.</font>

47
00:03:26,610 --> 00:03:33,251
<font color="#000000">The take-home: CCS succeeds because of the
displacement information in its contrast-pair data,</font>

48
00:03:33,331 --> 00:03:35,396
<font color="#000000">not its specific loss formula.</font>

49
00:03:35,476 --> 00:03:40,687
<font color="#000000">A simple Midpoint-Displacement loss reproduces CCS
and, retuned, beats it.</font>

50
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:43,118
<font color="#000000">The exact loss is interchangeable;</font>

51
00:03:43,198 --> 00:03:47,480
<font color="#000000">the real lever is the displacement-versus-midpoint
trade-off.</font>
